window.__DATA=window.__DATA||{};window.__DATA['bank/2023_06_2']={"id":"2023_06_2","title":"2023年6月 第2套","year":2023,"month":6,"set":"2","readingOnly":false,"hasAudio":true,"audio":"audio/7692e7fc.mp3","audioDuration":1426.8,"audioMissing":false,"counts":{"total":55,"listening":25,"cloze":10,"matching":10,"reading":10},"passages":{"passage1":{"kind":"passage","text":"Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change. Supermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930. For some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu (即兴的)tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food. The typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way. In the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance (催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\""},"passage2":{"kind":"passage","text":"The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar. With the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday. But year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience. But don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them. The U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well."},"cloze":{"kind":"cloze","text":"with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Morocco is responding to increasing energy demands by setting up one of the largest solar plants in the world. The Noor solar power station is ___26___ in the city of Ouarzazate and, once completed, will generate 580 million watts of electricity. The World Bank estimates it will serve 1.1 million people. It's ___27___ to be completed soon. Morocco's current energy comes ___28___ from imports. The nation hopes to get 50 percent of its energy from renewable sources by 2030. With demand for energy ___29___ at an annual rate of 7 percent, the new solar plant could be a ___30___ part of that goal. \"This makes Morocco a big ___31___ in the field of solar energy in the Arab region and the African continent. It could also be a forerunner for many other countries in the world that ___32___ on foreign imports of energy,\" said Ali Hajji, a solar energy specialist and engineering professor. Experts believe that the Middle East and North Africa have huge ___33___ for solar energy projects. This is partly because of adequate sunlight and partly because technology has become more ___34___ in the region. \"The last few years have seen a realization of ___35___ how competitive solar technologies can be,\" said Michael Taylor, a senior analyst at the International Renewable Energy Agency.","wordBank":{"A":"affordable","B":"ancestor","C":"crucial","D":"depend","E":"initial","F":"insist","G":"just","H":"located","I":"mostly","J":"operating","K":"perhaps","L":"pioneer","M":"potential","N":"rising","O":"scheduled"}},"matching":{"kind":"matching","text":"","paragraphs":{"A":"For the past four decades, the leader of Formula One car racing, one of the biggest annual sporting series in the world, was Bernie Ecclestone, a former motorcycle parts dealer who built it into an international presence essentially on his own.","B":"A skilled backroom operator who speaks without a filter, Ecclestone said often that in his opinion, the sport was at its best when he was allowed to act as \"a dictator.”","C":"Yet now the dictator is gone. After an American company, Liberty Media, acquired the Formula One competition recently, Chase Carey—a former executive with Fox Broadcasting Company and DirecTV who by his own admission is not a fierce racing fan—was named to replace Ecclestone and to try to renovate the organization's management, reach and ambition.","D":"Among the goals, Carey said in an interview on Tuesday, is one that just about every global sport seems interested in chasing: increasing interest in the United States. \"People have said we're going to \"Americanize' it,\" Carey said. \"And we're not going to do that totally. But realistically, there are some elements of Americanization that the sport could use.\"","E":"While Formula One commands enormous audiences throughout much of the world, many American sports fans know it as that other motorsport, the one that is not Nascar (纳斯卡车赛). Formula One teams race far more technologically advanced vehicles around tracks all over the world-in magnificent events in places like Malaysia, Monaco, Singapore and the United Arab Emirates, and on tradition-rich tracks like Silverstone in England and Monza in Italy too.","F":"The series has an annual race in Austin, Texas. But within \"a few years,\" Carey said, he plans to bring another to a destination American city, like New York, Los Angeles, Miami or Las Vegas. Carey's ambitious plan is two-fold: first, change the business model of Formula One, which he said was a \"one-man show\" under Ecclestone that had a largely narrow vision when it came to negotiating partnership deals; and second, alter the way fans experience the sport, both in person and remotely, so that connections between the audience and people within the series are easier to make.","G":"Increased digital access for fans, a more behind-the-scenes experience for broadcast viewers and innovation in areas like virtual reality-what is it like to speed around a track inside a Ferrari?-are among the possibilities. \"The sport has clearly been underserved,\" Carey said. \"It doesn't do anything digitally. There's no marketing. It doesn't tell any stories. The goal in this is to make the fans connect to the live experience as much as possible, and the tools you have to do that, we're not using at all.\"","H":"The larger question, though, is a familiar one: Is there room for Formula One in the ever-crowded sports landscape of the United States? Opinions vary, particularly because viewing habits among consumers continue to evolve. John Bloom, a professor at Shippensburg University who has studied American sports history, said the biggest challenge for any sport trying to increase its presence in the United States was framing itself in a way that had lasting appeal. \"Sports generally become popular in some way because they establish a narrative,\" Bloom said. \"When I think of motorsports in the U.S., what we all think of is Nascar, and the narrative of Nascar is sort of rural, white, working-class Americans, mostly in the South, connecting with the atmosphere of those races. That's the narrative. When I think of the narrative of Formula One, it's a very different kind of audience.\"","I":"That difference, Carey said, is significant. While some might immediately link Formula One to Nascar in terms of American growth, Carey said Formula One's brand research had indicated there was very little crossover; rather, Formula One fans generally cite other so-called elite events, like Wimbledon or the Ryder Cup, as competitions they enjoy. \"Other than they're both cars, the Nascar fan base is a very different fan base,\" Carey said. \"It's a very regional fan base. Formula One is a global, famous brand of stars. These are machines that shock and awe you.\"","J":"Carey's background is in deal making and innovation. At Fox Broadcasting Company, he was a top advisor for years, known for his skill in helping to lead the launch of the company into sports, as well as the start of Fox News Channel. After going to DirecTV, he positioned the satellite provider as a mainstream option in millions of households.","K":"Now, after Liberty Media paid $4.4 billion to acquire Formula One, he is charged with making the investment pay off. \"I think they can build Formula One in the U.S.,\" said Patrick Crakes, an executive at In Vivo Media Group who spent 25 years at Fox Broadcasting Company before leaving in 2016 as a senior manager at Fox Sports. \"People don't work on their cars anymore. They don't want that connection anymore. It's about technology and pushing the limits. It's about speed, danger and risk. And Formula One has that more than any other racing series.\"","L":"That is what hooked Carey, and he said he thought his experience was not unusual. He recalled attending Formula One's Monaco race last year and being overwhelmed by the ceremony leading up to the event, the way the race charmed the city for days ahead of the start. In his mind, it felt like a Super Bowl （超 级碗橄榄球赛). M) Then, on race day, he watched as the cars rocketed out of a tunnel and went screaming toward a tight turn with the city's harbor and the Mediterranean Sea in the background framing the scene. He was fascinated. \"You can't help but be awed,\" he said, \"and I think that feeling can be translated to the viewer.\" N) He added: \"The broader sport is a little too inward-looking, and we need to be more open. In some ways, I'm glad to be coming from the outside. The guys who are in the sport forever are sitting there saying: 'We can't do that. We can't do that because it's never been done that way.\"\""},"letters":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","I","J","K","L"]}},"questions":[{"no":1,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"1","options":{"A":"A man was bitten by a snake.","B":"A man was taken to a hospital.","C":"A man kept a 4-foot snake as a pet.","D":"A man fell off his toilet seat."},"script":"Police have reported a man being bitten by a 4-foot snake when he lifted his toilet seat on Sunday morning.\nThe snake was not poisonous, so the man did not have to go to the hospital, but his arm was medically treated at the scene.\nThe police officer interviewed told news reporters the snake didn't belong to the man and it was unclear how it got into the apartment.\nThe snake was eventually captured by the local animal handlers and taken to an animal hospital afterward. It was being treated for an outer skin infection. It was unclear who the owner was. The snake may be taken to a local zoo for care if the owner does not come forward.\nOver the years, other creatures like frogs and fish have found their way into toilets. But this was the first instance of a snake in recent years.","audioStart":35.5,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"A","expl":"A man was bitten by a snake. 详解：细节辨认题。新闻开头明确指出，警方报告称周日上午一名男子在提起马桶座圈时被一条4英尺长的蛇咬伤。蛇无毒，男子未去医院，但手臂在现场接受了医疗处理。选项B（男子被送往医院）与原文不符，C（男子养4英尺长的蛇当宠物）和D（男子从马桶座上摔下来）均未在新闻中提及，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中的细节捕捉能力，重点关注事件核心要素（人物、动作、对象）"},{"no":2,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"2","options":{"A":"Where the snake had been taken.","B":"Whether the snake was infected.","C":"How the snake was captured.","D":"Who owned the snake."},"script":"Police have reported a man being bitten by a 4-foot snake when he lifted his toilet seat on Sunday morning.\nThe snake was not poisonous, so the man did not have to go to the hospital, but his arm was medically treated at the scene.\nThe police officer interviewed told news reporters the snake didn't belong to the man and it was unclear how it got into the apartment.\nThe snake was eventually captured by the local animal handlers and taken to an animal hospital afterward. It was being treated for an outer skin infection. It was unclear who the owner was. The snake may be taken to a local zoo for care if the owner does not come forward.\nOver the years, other creatures like frogs and fish have found their way into toilets. But this was the first instance of a snake in recent years.","audioStart":35.5,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"D","expl":"Who owned the snake. 详解：细节辨认题。新闻中警方提到蛇并非该男子所有，尚不清楚它如何进入公寓，随后进一步明确\"it was unclear who the owner was\"（蛇的主人是谁仍不清楚）。选项A（蛇被带到了哪里）、B（蛇是否被感染）、C（蛇如何被捕获）均非新闻中明确提到的\"未明确事项\"，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中对疑问信息的捕捉，关注原文中的\"unclear\"等提示词"},{"no":3,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"3","options":{"A":"Taking her trash out in fancy dresses.","B":"Amusing herself by going to ball parties.","C":"Sharing her photos with famous movie stars.","D":"Posting her daughter's photos on social media."},"script":"Every week since last April, Andrea Belcher has been doing something slightly unusual and amusing when she takes out her trash.\nIt all started off when a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a ball gown. This inspired Andrea to get dressed up to take out the trash.\nShe was too embarrassed to do it during the day. So one dark evening, she put on her black off-the-shoulder gown and put her trash bin out. Her daughter filmed it and the whole family laughed, but it seemed to go down well.\nThe following weeks, she found other outfits in her dressing-up box, and dressed up as different characters and famous people, sharing her exploits on social media. Before long, people started offering up their own fancy dresses for her to wear. And before she knew it, she had done 20 weeks.\nExplaining why she has kept up the videos, Andrea explained how nice it was to have a little bit of fun and craziness that made people smile. Besides, it also helps to remind people in the community to put out their trash bins each week.","audioStart":133.0,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"A","expl":"Taking her trash out in fancy dresses. 详解：细节推断题。新闻开头指出，自去年4月以来，安德里亚·贝尔彻每周倒垃圾时都会做一些不寻常且有趣的事。后续具体说明，受电影明星穿舞会礼服倒垃圾的照片启发，她会盛装打扮（穿晚礼服、扮演不同角色和名人）倒垃圾，并将视频分享到社交媒体。选项B（通过参加舞会自娱自乐）、C（与著名影星分享照片）、D（在社交媒体发布女儿照片）均与原文不符，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中的事件细节捕捉，结合上下文推断核心行为"},{"no":4,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"4","options":{"A":"To make herself popular.","B":"To amuse people.","C":"To please her daughter.","D":"To record her achievements."},"script":"Every week since last April, Andrea Belcher has been doing something slightly unusual and amusing when she takes out her trash.\nIt all started off when a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a ball gown. This inspired Andrea to get dressed up to take out the trash.\nShe was too embarrassed to do it during the day. So one dark evening, she put on her black off-the-shoulder gown and put her trash bin out. Her daughter filmed it and the whole family laughed, but it seemed to go down well.\nThe following weeks, she found other outfits in her dressing-up box, and dressed up as different characters and famous people, sharing her exploits on social media. Before long, people started offering up their own fancy dresses for her to wear. And before she knew it, she had done 20 weeks.\nExplaining why she has kept up the videos, Andrea explained how nice it was to have a little bit of fun and craziness that made people smile. Besides, it also helps to remind people in the community to put out their trash bins each week.","audioStart":133.0,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"B","expl":"To amuse people. 详解：细节推断题。新闻最后安德里亚解释坚持拍视频的原因：\"it was nice to have a little bit of fun and craziness that made people smile\"（能有一点乐趣和疯狂让人们微笑是很美好的事），同时提到这也能提醒社区居民每周倒垃圾。选项A（让自己受欢迎）、C（取悦女儿）、D（记录自己的成就）均未在原文中提及，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中对人物行为动机的捕捉，关注原文中的直接引语"},{"no":5,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"5","options":{"A":"Eat as much as they want for $10.","B":"Have a chance of winning a $ 100 prize.","C":"Have a meal even if they have no money.","D":"Get a free meal after answering some questions."},"script":"No money but still hungry? No problem! Welcome to Frank's restaurant where customers are paying for meals and leaving receipts on a board. Anyone who is hungry and wants something to eat can take one, no questions asked and hand it to the staff for some food.\nThe idea for the board of free meals started after a donor came in with $100 for each member of its staff. After two staffers returned to their share, there was $700 leftover according to Tarah Morris, the owner of the restaurant, so they decided to do something more by creating the board.\nThe board of free meals inside the restaurant shows a host of potential meals already paid for by customers, each costing about $10. The board has messages of kindness like, \"If you want more kindness in the world, put it here.\"\nThe drive has been going on for two weeks and Morris said just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal. There have been no reports of anyone taking advantage of the situation. In fact, there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving. It seems people want to help and are driven by helping others.","audioStart":252.7,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"C","expl":"Have a meal even if they have no money. 详解：细节推断题。新闻开头明确提出\"No money but still hungry? No problem! Welcome to Frank's restaurant\"，随后解释餐厅规则：顾客付费后会将收据留在黑板上，饥饿且无钱的人可领取收据向工作人员兑换食物，无需提问。选项A（花10美元随意吃）、B（有机会赢得100美元奖金）、D（回答问题后获得免费餐）均与原文规则不符，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中对服务规则的细节捕捉，关注\"no questions asked\"等关键表述"},{"no":6,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"6","options":{"A":"It was brought up by two staffers.","B":"It helped to popularize her restaurant.","C":"It originated from a donation to her staff.","D":"It was suggested by some of her customers."},"script":"No money but still hungry? No problem! Welcome to Frank's restaurant where customers are paying for meals and leaving receipts on a board. Anyone who is hungry and wants something to eat can take one, no questions asked and hand it to the staff for some food.\nThe idea for the board of free meals started after a donor came in with $100 for each member of its staff. After two staffers returned to their share, there was $700 leftover according to Tarah Morris, the owner of the restaurant, so they decided to do something more by creating the board.\nThe board of free meals inside the restaurant shows a host of potential meals already paid for by customers, each costing about $10. The board has messages of kindness like, \"If you want more kindness in the world, put it here.\"\nThe drive has been going on for two weeks and Morris said just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal. There have been no reports of anyone taking advantage of the situation. In fact, there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving. It seems people want to help and are driven by helping others.","audioStart":252.7,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"C","expl":"详解：细节辨认题。新闻中餐厅老板塔拉·莫里斯提到，设立免费餐告示板的想法源于一位捐赠者为每位员工提供100美元，两名员工退回份额后剩余700美元，因此他们决定通过设立告示板做更多事。选项A（由两名员工提出）、B（帮助推广餐厅）、D（由部分顾客建议）均与原文不符，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中对事件起源的细节捕捉，关注\"started after\"等提示词"},{"no":7,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"7","options":{"A":"Fifty customers have offered donations.","B":"More people have been giving than taking.","C":"Many people have come to eat at the restaurant.","D":"Most staffers have received messages of kindness."},"script":"No money but still hungry? No problem! Welcome to Frank's restaurant where customers are paying for meals and leaving receipts on a board. Anyone who is hungry and wants something to eat can take one, no questions asked and hand it to the staff for some food.\nThe idea for the board of free meals started after a donor came in with $100 for each member of its staff. After two staffers returned to their share, there was $700 leftover according to Tarah Morris, the owner of the restaurant, so they decided to do something more by creating the board.\nThe board of free meals inside the restaurant shows a host of potential meals already paid for by customers, each costing about $10. The board has messages of kindness like, \"If you want more kindness in the world, put it here.\"\nThe drive has been going on for two weeks and Morris said just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal. There have been no reports of anyone taking advantage of the situation. In fact, there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving. It seems people want to help and are driven by helping others.","audioStart":252.7,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"B","expl":"More people have been giving than taking. 详解：细节推断题。新闻最后提到，免费餐行动已进行两周，仅不到50名顾客领取免费餐，且无人滥用该机制，\"there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving\"（送收据的人比拿收据的人多得多）。选项A（50名顾客提供了捐赠）、C（许多人来餐厅用餐）、D（大多数员工收到了善意信息）均与原文不符，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：新闻听力中对数量对比信息的捕捉，关注\"more...than...\"等结构 Section B"},{"no":8,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"8","options":{"A":"He is a psychologist.","B":"He is a famous writer.","C":"He is a host for a TV program.","D":"He is a primary school teacher."},"script":"W: Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships. Our guest today is psychologist Steve Pinker from McGill University.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Hi, Steve. Our question today is why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace.\nM: The answer may lie in childhood, the way the person related to their mom and dad. It's related to their attachment styles.\nW: Attachment styles?\nM: Yeah. There are two different attachment styles: an anxious attachment style and an avoidant attachment style. When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious attachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back. But other children simply shut down and try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation. And these attachment styles carry on into adulthood.\nW: Does avoidance reduce their stress levels?\nM: Not really. Their stress levels are actually the same as those experienced by the children who yell and scream.\nW: And I assume both might be termed insecure.\nM: Exactly. And it's a very insecure attachment.\nW: How does an insecure attachment affect relationships between friends, co-workers and romantic relationships?\nM: It's the same as conflicts. So if you have a disagreement with a securely attached person, they're not going to panic. They're able to hear what the other person is saying and express their own feelings and thoughts effectively. However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship.\nW: That explains why they suddenly disappear.","audioStart":428.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"A","expl":"详解：细节辨认题。对话开头女士介绍嘉宾：\"Our guest today is psychologist Steve Pinker from McGill University\"（今天的嘉宾是麦吉尔大学的心理学家史蒂夫·平克）。选项B（著名作家）、C（电视节目主持人）、D（小学教师）均与原文介绍不符，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：长对话中对人物身份的细节捕捉，关注开头的人物介绍"},{"no":9,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"9","options":{"A":"Why social media accounts vanish without a trace.","B":"Why parents raise their children in different ways.","C":"Why people fail to respond to emails promptly.","D":"Why friends break off contact all of a sudden."},"script":"W: Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships. Our guest today is psychologist Steve Pinker from McGill University.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Hi, Steve. Our question today is why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace.\nM: The answer may lie in childhood, the way the person related to their mom and dad. It's related to their attachment styles.\nW: Attachment styles?\nM: Yeah. There are two different attachment styles: an anxious attachment style and an avoidant attachment style. When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious attachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back. But other children simply shut down and try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation. And these attachment styles carry on into adulthood.\nW: Does avoidance reduce their stress levels?\nM: Not really. Their stress levels are actually the same as those experienced by the children who yell and scream.\nW: And I assume both might be termed insecure.\nM: Exactly. And it's a very insecure attachment.\nW: How does an insecure attachment affect relationships between friends, co-workers and romantic relationships?\nM: It's the same as conflicts. So if you have a disagreement with a securely attached person, they're not going to panic. They're able to hear what the other person is saying and express their own feelings and thoughts effectively. However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship.\nW: That explains why they suddenly disappear.","audioStart":428.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"D","expl":"Why friends break off contact all of a sudden. 详解：细节辨认题。对话中女士明确提出本期节目话题：\"why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace\"（为什么朋友会突然消失，他们可能在社交媒体上拉黑我们，不再回复邮件，毫无踪迹地消失）。后续男士围绕依恋风格展开分析，最后女士回应\"that explains why they suddenly disappear\"，进一步印证话题核心。选项A（社交媒体账号为何消失）、B（父母为何以不同方式养育孩子）、C（人们为何不及时回复邮件）均非对话核心话题，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：长对话中对核心话题的捕捉，关注开头的问题引入和结尾的总结呼应"},{"no":10,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"10","options":{"A":"They simply shut themselves down.","B":"They avoid showing their emotions.","C":"They scream to get their parents back.","D":"They attempt to ignore the whole situation."},"script":"W: Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships. Our guest today is psychologist Steve Pinker from McGill University.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Hi, Steve. Our question today is why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace.\nM: The answer may lie in childhood, the way the person related to their mom and dad. It's related to their attachment styles.\nW: Attachment styles?\nM: Yeah. There are two different attachment styles: an anxious attachment style and an avoidant attachment style. When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious attachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back. But other children simply shut down and try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation. And these attachment styles carry on into adulthood.\nW: Does avoidance reduce their stress levels?\nM: Not really. Their stress levels are actually the same as those experienced by the children who yell and scream.\nW: And I assume both might be termed insecure.\nM: Exactly. And it's a very insecure attachment.\nW: How does an insecure attachment affect relationships between friends, co-workers and romantic relationships?\nM: It's the same as conflicts. So if you have a disagreement with a securely attached person, they're not going to panic. They're able to hear what the other person is saying and express their own feelings and thoughts effectively. However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship.\nW: That explains why they suddenly disappear.","audioStart":428.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"C","expl":"They scream to get their parents back. 详解：细节辨认题。男士在解释依恋风格时提到：\"When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious attachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back\"（当与父母关系破裂时，焦虑型依恋的孩子会尖叫哭泣，让父母回来）。选项A（完全封闭自己）、B（避免表达情绪）、D（试图忽视整个情况）均是回避型依恋孩子的表现，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：长对话中对分类信息的捕捉，关注\"anxious attachment style\"等特定术语对应的行为描述"},{"no":11,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"11","options":{"A":"They may regard any difference as the end of a relationship.","B":"They are on better terms with friends and romantic partners.","C":"They try to express their feelings and thoughts effectively.","D":"They attach more value to their relationships with others."},"script":"W: Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships. Our guest today is psychologist Steve Pinker from McGill University.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Hi, Steve. Our question today is why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace.\nM: The answer may lie in childhood, the way the person related to their mom and dad. It's related to their attachment styles.\nW: Attachment styles?\nM: Yeah. There are two different attachment styles: an anxious attachment style and an avoidant attachment style. When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious attachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back. But other children simply shut down and try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation. And these attachment styles carry on into adulthood.\nW: Does avoidance reduce their stress levels?\nM: Not really. Their stress levels are actually the same as those experienced by the children who yell and scream.\nW: And I assume both might be termed insecure.\nM: Exactly. And it's a very insecure attachment.\nW: How does an insecure attachment affect relationships between friends, co-workers and romantic relationships?\nM: It's the same as conflicts. So if you have a disagreement with a securely attached person, they're not going to panic. They're able to hear what the other person is saying and express their own feelings and thoughts effectively. However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship.\nW: That explains why they suddenly disappear.","audioStart":428.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"A","expl":"详解：细节辨认题。男士提到：\"However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship\"（然而，缺乏安全感的人不会这样做。他们可能会攻击你、防御自己，或者直接从你的生活中消失。对他们来说，无论在你看来多么小的分歧，都预示着关系的结束）。选项B（与朋友和伴侣关系更好）、C（努力有效表达感受和想法）、D（更重视与他人的关系）均是安全型依恋者的表现，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：长对话中对对比信息的捕捉，关注\"insecurely attached person\"与\"securely attached person\"的行为差异"},{"no":12,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"12","options":{"A":"Their price.","B":"Their color.","C":"Their quality.","D":"Their design."},"script":"M: I hear you're going to Japan soon.\nW: Yes, that's right.\nM: Could I ask you a favor?\nW: Sure. What is it?\nM: I was wondering if you could get me some jeans while you're there. I'll pay you back, of course.\nW: A pair of jeans? Okay. I don't see why not. But what's wrong with jeans here?\nM: Well, I just love good jeans and Japan happens to make the best in the world. My friend brought me a pair from there five years ago, and they still look new.\nW: Really? I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans because it's a typical American garment.\nM: Yeah, I thought so too. But it turns out Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools used to make jeans the original way. It's a slower process of making clothing. But the end result is surely superior. Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural. They have a denser weave, and are more durable than any other jeans in the world.\nW: That sounds cool. What size do you want and how much are they?\nM: I have a 34 waist and size 30 or 32 in length. Either is fine. They should be around $500. But make double certain they are made in Japan.\nW: $500? Boy, that's expensive. Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers?\nM: They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several years and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it.","audioStart":634.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"C","expl":"Their quality. 详解：细节辨认题。男士请女士帮忙在日本买牛仔裤，解释原因：\"I just love good jeans and Japan happens to make the best in the world\"（我喜欢好的牛仔裤，而日本恰好做出了世界上最好的牛仔裤），后续补充提到日本牛仔裤面料质量更高、染料天然、更耐用。选项A（价格）、B（颜色）、D（设计）均未作为购买原因提及，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：长对话中对购物决策原因的捕捉，关注\"best\"、\"higher quality\"等质量相关表述"},{"no":13,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"13","options":{"A":"Jeans are a typical American garment.","B":". America makes the best-known brands of jeans.","C":"America has the best weaving tools in the world.","D":"Jeans are available in a greater variety in America."},"script":"M: I hear you're going to Japan soon.\nW: Yes, that's right.\nM: Could I ask you a favor?\nW: Sure. What is it?\nM: I was wondering if you could get me some jeans while you're there. I'll pay you back, of course.\nW: A pair of jeans? Okay. I don't see why not. But what's wrong with jeans here?\nM: Well, I just love good jeans and Japan happens to make the best in the world. My friend brought me a pair from there five years ago, and they still look new.\nW: Really? I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans because it's a typical American garment.\nM: Yeah, I thought so too. But it turns out Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools used to make jeans the original way. It's a slower process of making clothing. But the end result is surely superior. Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural. They have a denser weave, and are more durable than any other jeans in the world.\nW: That sounds cool. What size do you want and how much are they?\nM: I have a 34 waist and size 30 or 32 in length. Either is fine. They should be around $500. But make double certain they are made in Japan.\nW: $500? Boy, that's expensive. Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers?\nM: They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several years and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it.","audioStart":634.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"A","expl":"Jeans are a typical American garment. 详解：细节辨认题。女士对男士的请求表示困惑：\"I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans because it's a typical American garment\"（我不知道日本产牛仔裤，我本以为美国产的是最好的，因为牛仔裤是典型的美国服装）。选项B（美国有最知名的牛仔裤品牌）、C（美国有世界上最好的织造工具）、D（美国的牛仔裤款式更多样）均未在女士的困惑中提及，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：长对话中对人物困惑原因的捕捉，关注\"because\"引导的因果关系"},{"no":14,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"14","options":{"A":"They are artificial.","B":"They are natural.","C":"They are unique.","D":"They are special."},"script":"M: I hear you're going to Japan soon.\nW: Yes, that's right.\nM: Could I ask you a favor?\nW: Sure. What is it?\nM: I was wondering if you could get me some jeans while you're there. I'll pay you back, of course.\nW: A pair of jeans? Okay. I don't see why not. But what's wrong with jeans here?\nM: Well, I just love good jeans and Japan happens to make the best in the world. My friend brought me a pair from there five years ago, and they still look new.\nW: Really? I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans because it's a typical American garment.\nM: Yeah, I thought so too. But it turns out Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools used to make jeans the original way. It's a slower process of making clothing. But the end result is surely superior. Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural. They have a denser weave, and are more durable than any other jeans in the world.\nW: That sounds cool. What size do you want and how much are they?\nM: I have a 34 waist and size 30 or 32 in length. Either is fine. They should be around $500. But make double certain they are made in Japan.\nW: $500? Boy, that's expensive. Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers?\nM: They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several years and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it.","audioStart":634.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"They are natural. 详解：细节辨认题。男士介绍日本牛仔裤的优势时明确提到：\"the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural\"（它们使用的面料质量更高，染料是天然的）。选项A（人造的）、C（独特的）、D（特别的）均与原文描述不符，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：长对话中对产品特征的细节捕捉，关注并列结构中的具体信息"},{"no":15,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"15","options":{"A":"They are for casual wearing.","B":"They are popular with boys.","C":"They are much too pricey.","D":"They are worth the price."},"script":"M: I hear you're going to Japan soon.\nW: Yes, that's right.\nM: Could I ask you a favor?\nW: Sure. What is it?\nM: I was wondering if you could get me some jeans while you're there. I'll pay you back, of course.\nW: A pair of jeans? Okay. I don't see why not. But what's wrong with jeans here?\nM: Well, I just love good jeans and Japan happens to make the best in the world. My friend brought me a pair from there five years ago, and they still look new.\nW: Really? I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans because it's a typical American garment.\nM: Yeah, I thought so too. But it turns out Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools used to make jeans the original way. It's a slower process of making clothing. But the end result is surely superior. Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural. They have a denser weave, and are more durable than any other jeans in the world.\nW: That sounds cool. What size do you want and how much are they?\nM: I have a 34 waist and size 30 or 32 in length. Either is fine. They should be around $500. But make double certain they are made in Japan.\nW: $500? Boy, that's expensive. Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers?\nM: They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several years and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it.","audioStart":634.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"D","expl":"They are worth the price. 详解：细节辨认题。女士认为500美元一条牛仔裤太贵，男士回应：\"They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several years and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it\"（确实有点贵，但它们很特别。要知道，它们能穿好几年，而且也很时尚，我觉得完全值得这个价格）。选项A（适合休闲穿着）、B（受男孩欢迎）、C（太贵了）均非男士对日本牛仔裤的最终评价，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：长对话中对人物观点态度的捕捉，关注\"worth it\"等总结性表述 Section C"},{"no":16,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"16","options":{"A":"He desires more in life.","B":"He wants to see it again.","C":"He feels as inspired as other audience members.","D":"He longs to become a superstar himself."},"script":"Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same way. I experience a sense of longing, realizing that there should be more in life. The desire for adventure is a human one. Superhero films capture this desire perfectly.\nThe plot of each superhero film has become relatively predictable: the world is in peril, the world needs a hero, the hero is in peril, the hero resolves the peril, the hero saves the world. Each film has an attractive lead, several expensive explosions, a few jokes to lighten the mood, and numerous punches that land unrealistically well. Even though we basically know what will happen, we keep coming back for more.\nThe elites of our society have rejected superhero films. They say the films lack any form of artistry. These movies are criticized as being too simple. But they give the fans what they want—entertainment.\nNo matter the form in which they reveal themselves, hero stories will always be present in life. It's not just about being entertained, but also being inspired. One author has mentioned that women desire to play an irreplaceable role in a great adventure. \"Most young women want to be a part of something grand, something important.\" It seems clear that this principle applies to men, as well.\nSo next time you see your favorite superhero film, be entertained, but also be inspired. Let that small longing you feel motivate you to make a difference.","audioStart":862.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"A","expl":"He desires more in life. 详解：细节辨认题。短文开头提到：\"Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same way. I experience a sense of longing, realizing that there should be more in life\"（每次看完超级英雄电影，我都有同样的感受，一种渴望油然而生，意识到生活中应该有更多可能）。选项B（想再看一遍）、C（和其他观众一样受启发）、D（渴望自己成为超级巨星）均与原文不符，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对人物感受的细节捕捉，关注\"longing\"、\"realizing\"等情感提示词"},{"no":17,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"17","options":{"A":"It is rather unrealistic.","B":"It is extremely artistic.","C":"It is somewhat complicated.","D":"It is relatively predictable."},"script":"Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same way. I experience a sense of longing, realizing that there should be more in life. The desire for adventure is a human one. Superhero films capture this desire perfectly.\nThe plot of each superhero film has become relatively predictable: the world is in peril, the world needs a hero, the hero is in peril, the hero resolves the peril, the hero saves the world. Each film has an attractive lead, several expensive explosions, a few jokes to lighten the mood, and numerous punches that land unrealistically well. Even though we basically know what will happen, we keep coming back for more.\nThe elites of our society have rejected superhero films. They say the films lack any form of artistry. These movies are criticized as being too simple. But they give the fans what they want—entertainment.\nNo matter the form in which they reveal themselves, hero stories will always be present in life. It's not just about being entertained, but also being inspired. One author has mentioned that women desire to play an irreplaceable role in a great adventure. \"Most young women want to be a part of something grand, something important.\" It seems clear that this principle applies to men, as well.\nSo next time you see your favorite superhero film, be entertained, but also be inspired. Let that small longing you feel motivate you to make a difference.","audioStart":862.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"D","expl":"详解：细节辨认题。短文中明确指出：\"The plot of each superhero film has become relatively predictable, the world is in peril, the world needs a hero, the hero is in peril, the hero resolves the peril, the hero saves the world\"（每部超级英雄电影的情节都变得相对可预测：世界陷入危机，需要英雄，英雄陷入危机，英雄化解危机，拯救世界）。选项A（相当不切实际）、B（极具艺术性）、C（有些复杂）均与原文描述不符，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对事物特征的细节捕捉，关注直接描述性语句"},{"no":18,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"18","options":{"A":"They are biased against women.","B":"They are basically misleading.","C":"They are full of shootings.","D":"They are too simple."},"script":"Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same way. I experience a sense of longing, realizing that there should be more in life. The desire for adventure is a human one. Superhero films capture this desire perfectly.\nThe plot of each superhero film has become relatively predictable: the world is in peril, the world needs a hero, the hero is in peril, the hero resolves the peril, the hero saves the world. Each film has an attractive lead, several expensive explosions, a few jokes to lighten the mood, and numerous punches that land unrealistically well. Even though we basically know what will happen, we keep coming back for more.\nThe elites of our society have rejected superhero films. They say the films lack any form of artistry. These movies are criticized as being too simple. But they give the fans what they want—entertainment.\nNo matter the form in which they reveal themselves, hero stories will always be present in life. It's not just about being entertained, but also being inspired. One author has mentioned that women desire to play an irreplaceable role in a great adventure. \"Most young women want to be a part of something grand, something important.\" It seems clear that this principle applies to men, as well.\nSo next time you see your favorite superhero film, be entertained, but also be inspired. Let that small longing you feel motivate you to make a difference.","audioStart":862.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"D","expl":"They are too simple. 详解：事实细节题。短文提到：\"The elites of our society have rejected superhero films. They say the films lack any form of artistry. These movies are criticized as being too simple\"（社会精英拒绝看超级英雄电影，他们认为这些电影缺乏艺术性，被批评为过于简单）。选项A（对女性有偏见）、B（基本上具有误导性）、C（充满枪战）均未在原文中提及，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对他人观点的捕捉，关注\"rejected\"、\"criticized\"等态度提示词"},{"no":19,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"19","options":{"A":"It can highlight leadership.","B":"It can help connect people.","C":"It is a means to inspire creative thinking.","D":"It is an intuitive way to solidify friendship."},"script":"Telling stories is one of the most powerful means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire. Storytelling forges connections among people. When it comes to our countries, our communities, and our families, we understand instinctively that the stories we hold in common are an important part of the ties that bind.\nThis understanding also holds true in the business world, where an organization's stories, and the stories its leaders tell, help solidify relationships in a way that factual statements don't.\nGood stories do more than create a sense of connection. They build familiarity and trust. They allow the listener to enter the story, making them more open to learning. Good stories can contain multiple meanings, so they're surprisingly economical in conveying complex ideas in graspable ways. And stories are more engaging than the recital of data points or a discussion of abstract ideas.\nStories about professional mistakes and what leaders learned from them are another great avenue for learning. Because people identify so closely with stories, they can imagine how they would have acted in similar circumstances. They're able to work through situations in a way that's risk-free. There are extra benefits for leaders: with a simple personal story, they can convey fundamental values. They can offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge, presenting themselves as more approachable and then they are more likely to inspire others to want to know more.","audioStart":1038.7,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"B","expl":"It can help connect people. 详解：细节辨认题。短文开头指出：\"Storytelling forges connections among people\"（讲故事能建立人与人之间的联系），后续补充：\"Good stories do more than create a sense of connection. They build familiarity and trust\"（好的故事不仅能创造联系感，还能建立熟悉感和信任感）。选项A（能突出领导力）、C（是激发创造性思维的手段）、D（是巩固友谊的直观方式）均非原文中对讲故事作用的核心表述，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对核心观点的捕捉，关注开头的主题句"},{"no":20,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"20","options":{"A":"Allow them to recite data points.","B":"Make them more open to learning.","C":"Enable them to remember the main idea.","D":"Stimulate them to engage in discussions."},"script":"Telling stories is one of the most powerful means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire. Storytelling forges connections among people. When it comes to our countries, our communities, and our families, we understand instinctively that the stories we hold in common are an important part of the ties that bind.\nThis understanding also holds true in the business world, where an organization's stories, and the stories its leaders tell, help solidify relationships in a way that factual statements don't.\nGood stories do more than create a sense of connection. They build familiarity and trust. They allow the listener to enter the story, making them more open to learning. Good stories can contain multiple meanings, so they're surprisingly economical in conveying complex ideas in graspable ways. And stories are more engaging than the recital of data points or a discussion of abstract ideas.\nStories about professional mistakes and what leaders learned from them are another great avenue for learning. Because people identify so closely with stories, they can imagine how they would have acted in similar circumstances. They're able to work through situations in a way that's risk-free. There are extra benefits for leaders: with a simple personal story, they can convey fundamental values. They can offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge, presenting themselves as more approachable and then they are more likely to inspire others to want to know more.","audioStart":1038.7,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"B","expl":"Make them more open to learning. 详解：细节辨认题。短文中提到：\"They allow the listener to enter the story, making them more open to learning\"（好的故事能让听众沉浸其中，使他们更愿意学习）。选项A（允许他们背诵数据点）、C（让他们记住主旨）、D（刺激他们参与讨论）均与原文不符，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对事物作用的细节捕捉，关注\"making them...\"等结果状语"},{"no":21,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"21","options":{"A":"Inspire listeners' imagination.","B":"Enrich their own experience.","C":"Convey fundamental values.","D":"Explain insightful ideas."},"script":"Telling stories is one of the most powerful means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire. Storytelling forges connections among people. When it comes to our countries, our communities, and our families, we understand instinctively that the stories we hold in common are an important part of the ties that bind.\nThis understanding also holds true in the business world, where an organization's stories, and the stories its leaders tell, help solidify relationships in a way that factual statements don't.\nGood stories do more than create a sense of connection. They build familiarity and trust. They allow the listener to enter the story, making them more open to learning. Good stories can contain multiple meanings, so they're surprisingly economical in conveying complex ideas in graspable ways. And stories are more engaging than the recital of data points or a discussion of abstract ideas.\nStories about professional mistakes and what leaders learned from them are another great avenue for learning. Because people identify so closely with stories, they can imagine how they would have acted in similar circumstances. They're able to work through situations in a way that's risk-free. There are extra benefits for leaders: with a simple personal story, they can convey fundamental values. They can offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge, presenting themselves as more approachable and then they are more likely to inspire others to want to know more.","audioStart":1038.7,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"C","expl":"详解：细节推断题。短文末尾提到：\"with a simple personal story, they can convey fundamental values\"（通过一个简单的个人故事，领导者可以传达基本的价值观）。选项A（激发听众的想象力）、B（丰富自己的经历）、D（解释深刻的想法）均未在原文中提及，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对特定人群（领导者）行为作用的捕捉，关注\"extra benefits for leaders\"等提示语"},{"no":22,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"22","options":{"A":"Immigrants outnumber U.S.-born Americans.","B":"Immigrants have been contributing to the U.S.","C":"Another wave of immigrants is hitting the U.S.","D":"The number of immigrants to the U.S. is declining."},"script":"The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still contributing to America today—statistics prove it.\nOn two key measures of financial performance, immigrants outperform native-born Americans.\nFirst, they are more likely to be entrepreneurs. From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-born individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding. Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the child of one.\nAnother key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too. Immigrants use credit less often for purchasing cars, homes or starting businesses. This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans. For example, 34% of Americans born in Latin America report no credit card or installment loan debt. This can be compared to just 19% of the general population.\nWhy are immigrants doing so well? The values and habits they bring from their own homelands help explain some of the success.\nUnemployment tends to be higher in developing countries. That means more people must become entrepreneurs. Credit is also scarcer in most developing countries. This means individuals must save more in order to achieve their goals, or they borrow money from friends and family rather than a bank. That means less debt and potentially better credit outcomes when they reach the U.S.","audioStart":1215.6,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"B","expl":"详解：事实细节题。短文开头明确指出：\"The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still contributing to America today - statistics prove it\"（美国由一波又一波的移民建立，统计数据证明，移民至今仍在为美国做贡献）。选项A（移民数量超过美国本土出生人口）、C（另一波移民潮正在冲击美国）、D（美国移民数量在下降）均与原文不符，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对核心事实的捕捉，关注开头的总括性语句"},{"no":23,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"23","options":{"A":"More of them expect their children to succeed in business.","B":"They have fewer chances to be hired by U.S. companies.","C":"They have founded most Fortune 500 companies.","D":"More of them are successful business people."},"script":"The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still contributing to America today—statistics prove it.\nOn two key measures of financial performance, immigrants outperform native-born Americans.\nFirst, they are more likely to be entrepreneurs. From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-born individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding. Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the child of one.\nAnother key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too. Immigrants use credit less often for purchasing cars, homes or starting businesses. This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans. For example, 34% of Americans born in Latin America report no credit card or installment loan debt. This can be compared to just 19% of the general population.\nWhy are immigrants doing so well? The values and habits they bring from their own homelands help explain some of the success.\nUnemployment tends to be higher in developing countries. That means more people must become entrepreneurs. Credit is also scarcer in most developing countries. This means individuals must save more in order to achieve their goals, or they borrow money from friends and family rather than a bank. That means less debt and potentially better credit outcomes when they reach the U.S.","audioStart":1215.6,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"D","expl":"详解：细节推断题。短文中提到：\"First, they are more likely to be entrepreneurs. From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-born individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding\"（首先，他们更有可能成为企业家。1996年至2016年，移民创业的速度是美国本土出生人口的两倍，而且他们往往能取得成功）。选项A（更多人期望孩子在商界成功）、B（被美国公司雇佣的机会更少）、C（创办了大多数财富500强公司）均与原文不符（原文提到40%的财富500强公司创始人是移民或移民子女），故答案为D。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对数据和事实的综合推断，关注\"more likely\"、\"succeeding\"等表述"},{"no":24,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"24","options":{"A":"They have higher installment loan debt than native-born Americans.","B":"Nineteen percent of them borrow money from friends and family.","C":"Their level of debt is lower than that of native-born Americans.","D":"Thirty-four percent of them use credit for their daily purchases."},"script":"The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still contributing to America today—statistics prove it.\nOn two key measures of financial performance, immigrants outperform native-born Americans.\nFirst, they are more likely to be entrepreneurs. From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-born individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding. Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the child of one.\nAnother key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too. Immigrants use credit less often for purchasing cars, homes or starting businesses. This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans. For example, 34% of Americans born in Latin America report no credit card or installment loan debt. This can be compared to just 19% of the general population.\nWhy are immigrants doing so well? The values and habits they bring from their own homelands help explain some of the success.\nUnemployment tends to be higher in developing countries. That means more people must become entrepreneurs. Credit is also scarcer in most developing countries. This means individuals must save more in order to achieve their goals, or they borrow money from friends and family rather than a bank. That means less debt and potentially better credit outcomes when they reach the U.S.","audioStart":1215.6,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"C","expl":"Their level of debt is lower than that of native-born Americans. 详解：细节辨认题。短文中提到：\"Another key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too. Immigrants use credit less often... This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans\"（衡量财务实力的另一个关键指标是债务水平，移民在这方面也表现更好，他们较少使用信贷，导致债务水平明显低于美国本土出生人口）。选项A（分期付款债务比本土美国人高）、B（19%的人向亲友借钱）、D（34%的人日常消费使用信贷）均与原文不符（原文提到34%的拉丁美洲出生的美国移民没有信用卡或分期付款债务，而普通人口中这一比例仅为19%），故答案为C。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对对比信息的捕捉，关注\"lower than\"等比较结构"},{"no":25,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"25","options":{"A":"Keep their traditional values and old habits.","B":"Find employment in competitive businesses.","C":"Borrow money from financial institutions.","D":"Collaborate with native-born Americans."},"script":"The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still contributing to America today—statistics prove it.\nOn two key measures of financial performance, immigrants outperform native-born Americans.\nFirst, they are more likely to be entrepreneurs. From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-born individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding. Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the child of one.\nAnother key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too. Immigrants use credit less often for purchasing cars, homes or starting businesses. This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans. For example, 34% of Americans born in Latin America report no credit card or installment loan debt. This can be compared to just 19% of the general population.\nWhy are immigrants doing so well? The values and habits they bring from their own homelands help explain some of the success.\nUnemployment tends to be higher in developing countries. That means more people must become entrepreneurs. Credit is also scarcer in most developing countries. This means individuals must save more in order to achieve their goals, or they borrow money from friends and family rather than a bank. That means less debt and potentially better credit outcomes when they reach the U.S.","audioStart":1215.6,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"A","expl":"详解：细节推断题。短文最后解释移民成功的原因：\"The values and habits they bring from their own homelands help explain some of the success\"（他们从祖国带来的价值观和习惯有助于解释这些成功），后续补充提到发展中国家的环境使他们更倾向于创业、储蓄，而非依赖信贷。选项B（在竞争性企业中找到工作）、C（向金融机构借钱）、D（与美国本土出生人口合作）均未在原文中提及，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：短文听力中对原因的捕捉，关注\"help explain\"等因果提示词 Part III Reading Comprehension Section A（选词填空）"},{"no":26,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 26 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"located","answer":"H","expl":"located 详解：语法判断：空格前是系动词is，空格后是介词in，需填入可构成\"be+____+in\"结构的词。语义判断：句子介绍努奥太阳能发电站的位置，\"located\"意为\"位于\"，符合句意\"努奥太阳能发电站位于瓦尔扎扎特\"，故答案为H。 涉及知识点：固定搭配（be located in）、词性辨析（形容词/动词过去分词）"},{"no":27,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 27 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"scheduled","answer":"O","expl":"scheduled 详解：语法判断：空格前是系动词is，空格后是to do不定式，需填入满足\"be+____+to do\"结构的词。语义判断：句子表达发电站的完工计划，\"scheduled\"意为\"计划的、预定的\"，符合句意\"预计不久后就会完工\"，故答案为O。 涉及知识点：固定结构（be scheduled to do）、语义搭配（工程进度相关表述）"},{"no":28,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 28 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"mostly","answer":"I","expl":"mostly 详解：语法判断：空格前有动词comes，后接介词短语，需填入副词作状语。语义判断：结合常识，摩洛哥能源资源匮乏，\"mostly\"意为\"主要地、通常\"，符合句意\"摩洛哥目前的能源主要来自进口\"，故答案为I。 涉及知识点：副词辨析、语义逻辑（能源来源的主次关系）"},{"no":29,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 29 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"rising","answer":"N","expl":"rising 详解：语法判断：空格所在部分是With引导的复合结构，需填入动词-ing形式、过去分词或形容词。语义判断：文章首段提到摩洛哥应对\"日益增长的能源需求\"，\"rising\"意为\"增长的\"，符合句意\"随着能源需求以每年7%的速度增长\"，故答案为N。 涉及知识点：With复合结构、语义呼应（与首段\"increasing energy demands\"对应）"},{"no":30,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 30 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"crucial","answer":"C","expl":"crucial 详解：语法判断：空格前是不定冠词a，后接名词part，需填入形容词。语义判断：太阳能发电站是摩洛哥实现能源目标的重要举措，\"crucial\"意为\"至关重要的\"，符合句意\"新的太阳能发电站可能是实现这一目标的关键部分\"，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：形容词辨析、语义逻辑（能源项目与目标的关系）"},{"no":31,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 31 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"pioneer","answer":"L","expl":"pioneer 详解：语法判断：空格前有不定冠词a和形容词big，后接介词词组，需填入名词。语义判断：下句提到\"it could also be a forerunner\"（也可能是一个先例），\"pioneer\"意为\"先锋、先驱\"，与\"forerunner\"同义，符合句意\"这使摩洛哥成为阿拉伯地区和非洲大陆太阳能领域的一个重要先锋\"，故答案为L。 涉及知识点：名词辨析、同义替换（pioneer与forerunner）"},{"no":32,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 32 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"depend","answer":"D","expl":"depend 详解：语法判断：空格所在部分是that引导的定语从句，that作主语，需填入动词作谓语，且能与on搭配。语义判断：摩洛哥能源主要依赖进口，\"depend on\"意为\"依赖\"，符合句意\"对于世界上许多依赖外国进口能源的国家来说，这也可能是一个先例\"，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：动词辨析、固定搭配（depend on）"},{"no":33,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 33 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"potential","answer":"M","expl":"potential 详解：语法判断：空格前有形容词huge，后接介词词组，需填入名词。语义判断：中东和北非地区阳光充足，适合发展太阳能项目，\"potential\"意为\"潜力\"，符合句意\"专家认为，中东和北非在开发太阳能项目方面有巨大的潜力\"，故答案为M。 涉及知识点：名词辨析、语义逻辑（自然条件与项目潜力的关系）"},{"no":34,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 34 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"affordable","answer":"A","expl":"详解：语法判断：空格前有系动词become，需填入形容词。语义判断：空格处与\"adequate sunlight\"并列，均为太阳能项目的利好条件，\"affordable\"意为\"价格合理的、负担得起的\"，符合句意\"部分原因在于充足的阳光，部分原因在于该地区的技术变得更便宜\"，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：形容词辨析、语义并列（技术与自然条件的共同优势）"},{"no":35,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 35 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"just","answer":"G","expl":"just 详解：语法判断：空格前有of，空格后是how引导的完整从句，需填入副词作状语。语义判断：句子强调人们对太阳能技术竞争力的认知，\"just\"在此处表示强调，意为\"确实、真正地\"，符合句意\"过去几年，人们确实意识到了太阳能技术的竞争力\"，故答案为G。 涉及知识点：副词辨析、语义强调（突出认知的转变） Section B（匹配题）"},{"no":36,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"36. Chase Carey believes greater use should be made of digital technology to make Formula One more accessible to its fans.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"G","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"digital technology\"和\"accessible to its fans\"定位到G段第一句和最后一句。精析：定位句提到\"增加数字观赛渠道\"、\"为广播观众提供更多幕后体验\"、\"虚拟现实创新\"，目标是\"让粉丝尽可能多地与现场体验联系起来\"。题干中的\"greater use\"对应原文\"increased\"，\"accessible\"对应原文\"access\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为G。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（关键词定位）、同义替换（digital technology=数字观赛渠道/虚拟现实；accessible=access）"},{"no":37,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"37. Chase Carey was deeply impressed by the ceremony preceding last year's Monaco race.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"L","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"ceremony\"和\"Monaco race\"定位到L段第二句。精析：定位句指出\"他回忆起去年观看一级方程式摩纳哥站比赛，比赛前的仪式令他大吃一惊\"。题干中的\"deeply impressed\"对应原文\"overwhelmed\"，\"preceding\"对应原文\"leading up to\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为L。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（专有名词定位）、同义替换（impressed=overwhelmed；preceding=leading up to）"},{"no":38,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"38. One of Chase Carey's goals is to make Formula One more appealing to Americans.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"D","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"goals\"和\"more appealing to Americans\"定位到D段第一句。精析：定位句指出\"凯里的其中一个目标是增加美国人对这项运动的兴趣\"。题干中的\"more appealing\"对应原文\"increasing interest\"，\"Americans\"对应原文\"in the United States\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（目标类关键词定位）、同义替换（appealing=interest；Americans=in the United States）"},{"no":39,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"39. A former motorbike","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"A","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"former motorbike parts dealer\"定位到A段。精析：定位段指出\"过去四十年，一级方程式领导人是伯尼·埃克莱斯顿，他曾是摩托车零件经销商，凭一己之力将其打造成国际赛事\"。题干中的\"former motorbike parts dealer\"对应原文\"former motorcycle parts dealer\"，\"forty years\"对应原文\"four decades\"，题干是对原文的细节归纳，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（职业类关键词定位）、同义替换（motorbike=motorcycle；forty years=four decades）"},{"no":40,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"40. Chase Carey thought the audience of Formula One could be made to share his feeling about the race.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"M","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"audience\"和\"share his feeling\"定位到M段末句。精析：定位句指出\"你会不由自主地感到敬畏，我认为这种感觉可以传达给观众\"。题干中的\"audience\"对应原文\"viewer\"，\"share his feeling\"对应原文\"be translated to\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为M。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（情感类关键词定位）、同义替换（share his feeling=be translated to；audience=viewer）"},{"no":41,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"41. Chase Carey used to serve as a top advisor for a major broadcasting company.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"J","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"top advisor\"和\"broadcasting company\"定位到J段第二句。精析：定位句指出\"在福克斯广播公司，他多年来一直担任高级顾问\"。题干中的\"top advisor\"直接对应原文，\"major broadcasting company\"对应原文\"Fox Broadcasting Company\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为J。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（职位类关键词定位）、同义替换（major broadcasting company=Fox Broadcasting Company）"},{"no":42,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"42. Chase Carey intends to make connections easier between the audience and the Formula One racers.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"F","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"connections\"、\"easier\"和\"audience\"定位到F段末句。精析：定位句指出\"改变粉丝体验方式，使观众与赛事参与者之间的联系更容易建立\"。题干中的\"easier\"直接对应原文，\"the Formula One racers\"对应原文\"people within the series\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为F。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（关系类关键词定位）、同义替换（racers=people within the series；connections=links）"},{"no":43,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"43. The new leader of Formula One admitted he was not super interested in car racing.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"C","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"not super interested in car racing\"定位到C段第二句。精析：定位句指出\"蔡斯·凯里被任命接替埃克莱斯顿，他自己承认不是狂热的赛车迷\"。题干中的\"The new leader of Formula One\"对应原文\"Chase Carey... was named to replace Ecclestone\"，\"admitted\"对应原文\"admission\"，\"not super interested in car racing\"对应原文\"not a fierce racing fan\"，题干是对原文的细节归纳，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（态度类关键词定位）、同义替换（not super interested=not a fierce fan；admitted=admission）"},{"no":44,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"44. People's opinions differ as to whether Formula One can be promoted in the U.S.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"H","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"opinions\"和\"whether Formula One can be promoted\"定位到H段第一、二句。精析：定位句指出\"在拥挤的美国体育界，一级方程式是否有立足之地？人们各持己见\"。题干中的\"opinions differ\"对应原文\"Opinions vary\"，\"be promoted in the U.S.\"对应原文\"room for Formula One in the U.S. sports landscape\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为H。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（疑问类关键词定位）、同义替换（be promoted=have room；opinions differ=opinions vary）"},{"no":45,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"45. Compared with other racing series, Formula One focuses more on speed and involves more danger.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"K","expl":"定位：由题干中的\"speed\"和\"danger\"定位到K段倒数第一、二句。精析：定位句指出\"这关乎速度、危险和风险，一级方程式比其他任何系列赛都更具这些特点\"。题干中的\"Compared with other racing series\"对应原文\"more than any other racing series\"，题干是对原文的同义转述，故答案为K。 涉及知识点：定位技巧（核心特征关键词定位）、同义替换（focus more on=more than any other；speed and danger=speed, danger and risk） Section C（阅读理解） Passage One"},{"no":46,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"46. What problem have supermarkets been facing?","options":{"A":"They are actually on the way to bankruptcy.","B":"They have been losing customers and profits.","C":"They are forced to use e-commerce strategies.","D":"They have difficulty adapting to climate change."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"They have been losing customers and profits. 详解：定位：由题干中的\"problem\"和\"facing\"定位到第一段。精析：定位段提到，超市长期利润微薄，是人们最不期待光顾和工作的地方之一；十多年来受电子商务巨头冲击，被指责导致美国人发胖、加剧气候变化。由此可推断，超市的顾客和利润一直在减少。避错：A（濒临破产）是过度推理，原文仅说利润微薄；C（被迫采用电子商务策略）错误，原文是受电子商务冲击而非采用其策略；D（难以适应气候变化）错误，原文是被指责加剧气候变化，而非自身难以适应。故答案为B。 涉及知识点：细节理解与推理判断，关注段落核心问题的归纳"},{"no":47,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"47. What does the passage say about the idea of a self-service grocery?","options":{"A":"It was put forward by King Kullen.","B":"It originated in the United States.","C":"It has been under constant debate.","D":"It proves revolutionary even today."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"详解：定位：由题干中的\"the idea of a self-service grocery\"定位到第二段第二句。精析：定位句明确指出\"自助式杂货店这一革命性想法最早起源于美国\"。避错：A（由金库伦超市提出）错误，原文说金库伦超市被认为是第一家超市，而非想法提出者；C（一直存在争议）错误，原文争议的是\"哪家是第一家超市\"，而非自助杂货店的想法；D（如今仍具革命性）错误，原文说\"当时是销售领域的革命\"，如今已无前沿感。故答案为B。 涉及知识点：细节辨认，关注具体信息的准确对应"},{"no":48,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"48. What did supermarkets do by adopting the Fordist factory approach?","options":{"A":"They modernized traditional groceries in many ways.","B":"They introduced cutting-edge layout of their stores.","C":"They improved the quality of the food they sold.","D":"They revolutionized the distribution of goods."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"D","expl":"详解：定位：由题干中的\"Fordist factory approach\"定位到第三段第三、四句。精析：定位句指出，超市模仿福特主义工厂的效率和标准化理念，将其重新构想为购物场所，\"在当时是销售领域的一场革命\"，即改变了商品（食物）的分销方式。避错：A（对传统杂货店进行多方面现代化改造）范围过大，原文仅说改变了销售方式；B（采用最先进的店面布局）原文未提及；C（改善食品质量）原文未提及。故答案为D。 涉及知识点：细节理解与同义替换，关注\"distribution\"与\"selling\"的对应"},{"no":49,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"49. What is the typical supermarket layout intended to do?","options":{"A":"Arouse customers' appetite to buy flowers, fruit and vegetables.","B":"Provide customers easy access to items they want to buy.","C":"Induce customers to make more unplanned purchases.","D":"Enable customers to have a more enjoyable shopping experience."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"C","expl":"Induce customers to make more unplanned purchases. 详解：定位：由题干中的\"typical supermarket layout intended to do\"定位到第四段。精析：定位段提到，超市将鲜花、水果等放在门口吸引食欲，将牛奶、鸡蛋等必需品放在最内侧，迫使顾客穿过更多区域，\"途中受到诱惑\"，其目的是增加非计划购买。避错：A（唤起购买鲜花、水果的欲望）错误，门口陈列是为了营造新鲜感，而非仅促进该类商品购买；B（方便顾客购买所需商品）与原文相反，必需品放内侧是为了增加顾客移动距离；D（提供更愉快的购物体验）并非布局目的，原文未提及。故答案为C。 涉及知识点：推理判断，关注超市布局的潜在目的"},{"no":50,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"50. What have people long feared about supermarkets?","options":{"A":"They use tricky strategies to promote their business.","B":"They are going to replace the local groceries entirely.","C":"They apply corporate black magic to the goods on display.","D":"They take advantage of the weaknesses of women shoppers."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"A","expl":"详解：定位：由题干中的\"long feared about supermarkets\"定位到最后一段第二句。精析：定位句指出，人们担心超市的\"分销革命\"用\"企业黑魔法\"操纵消费者欲望，后文以《隐藏的劝说者》为例，说明超市让女性在货架间徘徊、随机购物，即采用策略促进销售。避错：B（完全取代当地杂货店）原文未提及；C（对展示商品使用黑魔法）是对\"corporate black magic\"的字面误解，实际指营销手段；D（利用女性顾客的弱点）以偏概全，原文举例女性只是为了说明超市的营销策略，并非特指针对女性。故答案为A。 涉及知识点：推理判断，关注比喻性表达的实际含义 Passage Two"},{"no":51,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"51. Why did America's traditional school year have a three-month summer vacation?","options":{"A":"Students needed to help with farm work.","B":"Students needed time to learn necessary farming skills.","C":"The agricultural society then attached less importance to academics.","D":"America lagged behind other countries in making a scientific school calendar."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"Students needed to help with farm work. 详解：定位：由题干中的\"a three-month summer vacation\"定位到首段第一句。精析：定位句明确指出，传统学年的三个月暑假最初是在美国农业社会实施的，\"夏季的几个月需要从事农场工作\"。避错：B（学习农业技能）、C（农业社会不重视学术）、D（校历制定落后于其他国家）均未在原文中提及。故答案为A。 涉及知识点：细节辨认，关注历史背景类信息的准确对应"},{"no":52,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"52. What benefit will year-round schooling bring students in addition to improving their learning?","options":{"A":"It will help them get back to where their lessons started.","B":"It will enable them to absorb what they have learned.","C":"It will familiarize them with the school environment.","D":"It will strengthen their relationship with teachers."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"D","expl":"详解：定位：由题干中的\"benefit\"和\"year-round schooling\"定位到第三段首句。精析：第二段论述全年制学校的学术好处，第三段补充其他益处：\"在全年制学校里，老师和学生会形成更密切的关系\"。避错：A（回到课程开始的地方）是学术方面的好处，与题干\"in addition to improving their learning\"不符；B（吸收所学知识）原文未提及；C（熟悉学校环境）错误，原文是\"不会感到与学校环境脱节\"，而非\"熟悉环境\"。故答案为D。 涉及知识点：细节辨认，关注\"in addition to\"对应的非学术好处"},{"no":53,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"53. What do some childhood development experts believe about the long summer vacation?","options":{"A":"It meets students' need to study on their own.","B":"It enables students to learn about the outside world.","C":"It satisfies students' desire to stay longer at home.","D":"It contributes to students' healthy growth."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"D","expl":"详解：定位：由题干中的\"childhood development experts\"定位到第四段第二句。精析：定位句指出，专家认为\"放假对孩子的健康发展至关重要\"，因为孩子不适合长时间待在教室，暑假提供了外出的绝佳机会。避错：A（满足独立学习需求）原文未提及；B（了解外部世界）是外出的可能结果，而非专家强调的核心（健康成长）；C（满足待在家里的愿望）与专家观点相反，专家认为暑假应外出。故答案为D。 涉及知识点：细节辨认，关注专家观点的核心诉求"},{"no":54,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"54. What is the argument against the experts' idea of a long summer vacation?","options":{"A":"It does little good to most students.","B":"It benefits few students playing outside.","C":"It leads students to neglect their studies.","D":"It makes students addicted to computer games."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"It does little good to most students. 详解：定位：由题干中的\"the argument against the experts' idea\"定位到第四段第三、四句。精析：定位句指出，专家观点的问题在于\"大多数孩子不出去玩，甚至不与其他孩子相处\"，多数孩子待在家里看电视、玩电子游戏，\"这对他们几乎没有好处\"。避错：B（对少数户外玩耍的孩子有益）是支持专家观点的论据，而非反对；C（忽视学业）、D（沉迷电脑游戏）是对\"看电视、玩电子游戏\"的过度推断，原文核心是\"几乎没有好处\"。故答案为A。 涉及知识点：细节辨认，关注反对意见的核心依据"},{"no":55,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"55. What does the author think of the traditional school year in the U.S. today?","options":{"A":"Well-grounded.","B":"Culture-bound.","C":"Outdated.","D":"Welcomed."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"Outdated. 详解：定位：由题干中的\"the traditional school year in the U.S. today\"定位到最后一段。精析：定位段指出，美国已从农业经济转变为知识和创新型经济，\"因此改变学年也是有意义的\"，言外之意是传统学年已不适应现状，即过时。避错：A（有充分依据的）、B（受文化限制的）、D（受欢迎的）均未在原文中提及。故答案为C。 涉及知识点：观点态度判断，关注作者的总结性表述"}],"warnings":[]};